menu "Powersuit bench node"

    config PS_BENCH_IS_ORCH
        bool "This board is the ORCHESTRATOR (heartbeat source)"
        default n
        help
            Off  = LIMB: runs the real safety state machine, shows its state on
                   the RGB LED, and trips to Passive Compliance when the beats
                   stop.
            On   = ORCHESTRATOR: emits the 100 Hz heartbeat and a 1 Hz
                   MODE_SET(OPERATIONAL).

            Flash one board each way. Pulling the orchestrator's USB lead is
            the dead-man switch test.

            A plain bool rather than a choice purely for simplicity — both
            override fine from an sdkconfig.defaults overlay PROVIDED the
            generated sdkconfig does not already exist. ESP-IDF treats an
            existing sdkconfig as authoritative and consults the defaults only
            for symbols it does not already contain, so a changed overlay is
            ignored on every build after the first. Delete the sdkconfig (or
            point -D SDKCONFIG at a fresh path) before switching roles, and
            read the generated file back to confirm the override landed.

    choice PS_BENCH_LED_REV
        prompt "DevKitC-1 revision (sets the onboard RGB LED pin)"
        default PS_BENCH_LED_BOTH
        help
            Espressif moved the onboard WS2812 between board revisions:
            GPIO48 on v1.0, GPIO38 on v1.1 (Espressif's user guides for each
            revision). The guides document the move, not the reason.

            Listings frequently do not say which revision they ship, so the
            default drives BOTH pins with the same colour: whichever carries the
            LED lights it, and the other is a plain header pin with nothing on
            it. Verified on this bench's boards by the sibling project, whose
            firmware does the same (docs/bench-log.md). Pick a single pin only
            if something else is wired to GPIO38 or GPIO48.

        config PS_BENCH_LED_BOTH
            bool "Either revision — drive GPIO38 and GPIO48"
        config PS_BENCH_LED_V11
            bool "v1.1 — GPIO38"
        config PS_BENCH_LED_V10
            bool "v1.0 — GPIO48"
        config PS_BENCH_LED_CUSTOM
            bool "Custom pin"
        config PS_BENCH_LED_NONE
            bool "No LED (log only)"
    endchoice

    config PS_BENCH_LED_GPIO_CUSTOM
        int "Custom RGB LED GPIO"
        depends on PS_BENCH_LED_CUSTOM
        default 38

    config PS_BENCH_LED_PROBE
        bool "LED probe at boot (identify the board revision)"
        default n
        help
            Drives GPIO38 and GPIO48 in turn for two seconds each, announcing
            which is active over serial. Whichever makes the LED light is your
            revision. One-time diagnostic: turn it off afterwards.

    config PS_BENCH_SELFTEST
        bool "CAN self-test instead of a role (one board, no wiring)"
        default n
        help
            Runs ps_can with the TWAI controller in self-test + loopback mode,
            RX on the TX pin, so it hears its own frames and needs no ACK: no
            transceiver, no second board. Checks that frames go out and come
            back, that a SAFETY frame queued behind telemetry overtakes it, that
            frames within a class keep their order, and the SAFETY latency.
            Prints SELFTEST PASS/FAIL lines; the LED goes steady green on pass,
            red strobe on fail. The role setting is ignored.

            Build with: BENCH_SELFTEST=1 bash firmware/tools/build_bench.sh

    config PS_BENCH_CAN_TX_GPIO
        int "TWAI TX GPIO"
        default 4
        help
            Safe on every S3 module: not a strapping pin, not bonded to flash
            or PSRAM (26-32, and 35-37 on Octal-PSRAM R8 parts), not USB
            (19/20), not UART0 console (43/44).

    config PS_BENCH_CAN_RX_GPIO
        int "TWAI RX GPIO"
        default 5

    config PS_BENCH_NODE_ID
        int "CAN node id (1-6 for a limb; 8 is the orchestrator)"
        default 1
        range 1 8

endmenu
